00:01:16.633 --> 00:01:17.666
Apple shortcuts

00:01:17.666 --> 00:01:20.666
is a platform that allows you automating stuff

00:01:20.733 --> 00:01:21.833
on your iPhone

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:25.966
For example, I use it for my morning automation

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:27.666
where it asks me for my weight,

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:29.800
if I ate supplements and so on.

00:01:30.066 --> 00:01:33.666
And then communicates with API to insert data

00:01:33.666 --> 00:01:36.966
into spreadsheets or mark to those tasks as done.

00:01:37.366 --> 00:01:40.700
In my case, it's mostly iPhone to server communication,

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:43.033
but there are tons of interactions that

00:01:43.033 --> 00:01:46.233
can be done between different apps on your phone.

00:01:47.233 --> 00:01:51.033
And your shortcuts are start using Apple's CloudKit.

00:01:51.133 --> 00:01:54.899
It's a cloud database storage that allows many apps

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:57.433
to save the user's data and authenticate

00:01:57.733 --> 00:02:00.800
directly from the phone using apple single sign on.

00:02:02.033 --> 00:02:05.233
And this data is stored inside containers,

00:02:05.366 --> 00:02:09.766
Apple shortcuts used COM.APPLE.SHORTCUTS container

00:02:10.466 --> 00:02:13.700
Inside, you have three different databases.

00:02:13.866 --> 00:02:17.300
Private to which only the owner has access.

00:02:17.366 --> 00:02:21.166
Shared which can be, well... shared with other users.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:24.633
and Public to which everyone has access.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:26.233
Normally,

00:02:26.233 --> 00:02:29.233
your shortcuts are stored in the Private database

00:02:29.233 --> 00:02:31.700
and you must authenticate as yourself

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:32.733
to access them.

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:37.066
Shortcuts can be very complex.

00:02:37.433 --> 00:02:38.833
To make things easier,

00:02:38.833 --> 00:02:42.566
you don't need to create all of them from scratch.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.633
There is a whole ecosystem of sharing them.

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:48.566
You can find and use shortcuts created by

00:02:48.566 --> 00:02:51.133
others on websites like RoutineHub.

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:54.866
Some of them include thousands of steps

00:02:55.433 --> 00:02:56.933
maybe you would think that

00:02:56.933 --> 00:02:58.833
Shared cloud database

00:02:58.833 --> 00:03:01.133
sounds like a perfect fit for that,

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:03.533
but apparently it wasn't.

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:05.766
When you want to share your shortcut

00:03:05.766 --> 00:03:07.700
a new record with type

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:11.566
shared shortcut is created in the public database,

00:03:11.933 --> 00:03:13.533
it gets a GUID

00:03:13.533 --> 00:03:14.400
and a link

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.233
which you can use to send it to other people.

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:20.400
You must know that this

00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:23.233
is the third vulnerability that Frans found

00:03:23.233 --> 00:03:25.233
and described in the blog post.

00:03:25.633 --> 00:03:29.600
Others allowed modifying the data on iCrowd website and

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:32.500
deleting articles from Apple news.

00:03:32.833 --> 00:03:36.733
So he had some ideas on how to work with CloudKit.

00:03:36.933 --> 00:03:37.733
It's important,

00:03:37.866 --> 00:03:39.900
because it's not always easy

00:03:39.966 --> 00:03:42.400
to query the CloudKit database.

00:03:43.233 --> 00:03:45.000
For example, the Notes app

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.966
would communicate with the CloudKit using a request

00:03:47.966 --> 00:03:49.133
that looks like this:

00:03:50.166 --> 00:03:52.533
While the request from the developer portal

00:03:52.566 --> 00:03:53.766
looked more like this:

00:03:55.233 --> 00:03:57.999
Apple Shortcuts on the other hand talked to

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:00.033
gateway.icloud.com

00:04:00.033 --> 00:04:01.866
Using protobuf messages

00:04:01.866 --> 00:04:04.900
which made it significantly harder to fiddle.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.900
However, it was possible to connect

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:10.466
to the shortcuts container

00:04:10.466 --> 00:04:13.333
by talking to the endpoint originally used

00:04:13.333 --> 00:04:14.966
by the developer portal.

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:18.966
What you needed to do is modifying the container name

00:04:19.066 --> 00:04:20.000
in the request.

00:04:20.566 --> 00:04:22.400
This way, it was possible

00:04:22.433 --> 00:04:24.800
to communicate with the shortcuts container

00:04:24.833 --> 00:04:26.900
using simple JSON bodies.

00:04:27.866 --> 00:04:31.966
The obvious and very impact attacks that come to mind

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:36.433
are adding modifying or deleting other users' shortcuts.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:39.233
However, access control

00:04:39.233 --> 00:04:42.666
was properly enforced on all those scenarios.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:45.466
And one more thing you need to know

00:04:45.466 --> 00:04:47.800
about CloudKit are zones.

00:04:48.233 --> 00:04:51.466
Public and shared databases can only use

00:04:51.533 --> 00:04:52.700
the default zone.

00:04:52.966 --> 00:04:55.033
But in private one, you can create

00:04:55.166 --> 00:04:58.600
new zones to group related objects together.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:02.366
At least, that's what the documentation says.

00:05:03.366 --> 00:05:04.466
But turned out

00:05:04.466 --> 00:05:07.500
that the public database in shortcuts container,

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:09.166
did use more than that.

00:05:09.566 --> 00:05:11.866
It also had metadata_zone.

00:05:12.733 --> 00:05:16.800
It was even possible to add new zones to the database,

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:18.766
but there was no impact to this

00:05:18.766 --> 00:05:21.766
as your zone wouldn't be used by anyone.

00:05:22.666 --> 00:05:25.466
You could also delete the zone you created,

00:05:25.466 --> 00:05:28.666
but only when authenticated as the same user.

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:31.533
It hinted that access control is

00:05:31.533 --> 00:05:34.100
properly enforced on those zones.

00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:37.700
But anyway, Frans wanted to test if it was

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:40.533
also possible to delete the metadata_zone.

00:05:41.233 --> 00:05:43.433
There was no other way to test this,

00:05:43.633 --> 00:05:45.666
other than trying to delete it.

00:05:46.233 --> 00:05:48.266
But he got an error saying

00:05:48.533 --> 00:05:52.000
user updates to system zones are not allowed

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:54.366
which was the next hint

00:05:54.366 --> 00:05:57.400
that access control is properly enforced here.

00:05:58.333 --> 00:06:01.933
But anyway, he tried to delete the _defaultZone as well.

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:03.200
It was the last

00:06:03.333 --> 00:06:06.133
idea he had for testing this functionality.

00:06:06.833 --> 00:06:07.800
And this time,

00:06:08.266 --> 00:06:09.266
it worked...

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:11.000
or it didn't?!

00:06:11.433 --> 00:06:14.700
Listing zones still returned the _defaultZone

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:16.600
like nothing was changed.

00:06:17.266 --> 00:06:20.233
So he started looking into other functionalities,

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:22.433
but he realized that

00:06:22.433 --> 00:06:25.033
one of his shared shortcuts was gone...

00:06:25.866 --> 00:06:28.033
So did the shortcut gallery...

00:06:28.566 --> 00:06:30.866
Shortcut sharing website as well...

00:06:32.233 --> 00:06:36.266
Turned out that all of the short shortcuts were deleted.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:41.466
Only those shared after the deletion were working.

00:06:41.933 --> 00:06:45.900
My guess is that the new _defaultZone was created just

00:06:46.033 --> 00:06:48.033
after the previous one was deleted.

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:51.600
Hence, it was present in the zone listing response.

00:06:52.133 --> 00:06:54.333
It was that much more problematic,

00:06:54.333 --> 00:06:55.933
because Apple couldn't just

00:06:55.933 --> 00:06:58.300
restore shortcuts from that moment,

00:06:58.433 --> 00:07:00.766
because the new ones would be then removed.

00:07:01.166 --> 00:07:03.966
They had to merge the old ones with the new ones,

00:07:03.966 --> 00:07:05.766
which took a few days.

00:07:06.633 --> 00:07:09.700
Obviously, this is something you never want to see

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:10.966
when bughunting.

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:14.766
Frans wrote an email to Apple a few minutes later

00:07:14.766 --> 00:07:16.433
explaining what happened.

00:07:17.633 --> 00:07:19.166
Later, he followed up

00:07:19.166 --> 00:07:21.100
clarifying the steps he took

00:07:21.100 --> 00:07:23.433
to prevent any service interruption,

00:07:23.433 --> 00:07:26.000
and try to explain how limited he was

00:07:26.233 --> 00:07:29.933
to confirm if the deletion call had worked or not.

00:07:30.833 --> 00:07:31.666
Thanks to that,

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:36.133
he got the bounty of $28,000 for this vulnerability.

00:07:36.866 --> 00:07:38.100
This shows that

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:40.933
even if you go a little too far with your testing,

00:07:40.933 --> 00:07:42.466
you can still get a reward

00:07:42.466 --> 00:07:45.133
if you justify that you did what you could

00:07:45.266 --> 00:07:46.300
to avoid that.

00:07:47.166 --> 00:07:49.266
Thank you for watching this episode!

00:07:49.266 --> 00:07:52.866
I wish your bugs will never cause such problems.

00:07:52.866 --> 00:07:54.400
If you enjoyed this video,

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:56.266
you may also like this one

00:07:56.266 --> 00:07:58.966
about 50k remote code execution

00:07:58.966 --> 00:08:00.933
in Apple Bug Bounty Program.

00:08:01.133 --> 00:08:03.600
For now, thank you for watching and goodbye!

